# Rooted quartet audit and independent external evidence.
(A/'quartet_null_audit.md').write_text('''# Frozen quartet null audit\n\n## Decision\n\nAll three designs are correctly rooted *four-population contrasts* in the formal sense that the eight *Mus spretus* samples are outside the three house-mouse populations and the stated P1/P2/P3 roles define ABBA/BABA orientation. They are **not validated conventional species-history nulls** for a clean D=0 test, and none is a genuine t-chromosome-specific comparison. Retain the frozen quartets as exploratory arrangement-associated population contrasts.\n\n## Sample/provenance audit\n\nThe frozen member table and source-backed mapping give D1 P1/P2 as 4/12 *M. m. domesticus* from France/Germany, P3 as 13 *M. m. musculus* from Afghanistan/Czechia/Kazakhstan, and O as eight *M. spretus* from Madrid. D2 is 8/13 *M. m. musculus* (t carriers sampled in Afghanistan, Czechia, Kazakhstan; standards from the same broad origins), 12 *M. m. domesticus* P3 from the France/Germany cohort, and the same eight outgroups. D3 is 3/7 *M. m. castaneus* from Himachal Pradesh, 13 *M. m. musculus* P3, and the same eight outgroups. The exact VCF/tree membership audit reports all 55 frozen IDs present in both sources; outgroup status is explicit in the frozen design. These are labels/provenance, not evidence that a population branching order is exchangeable.\n\n## Biological interpretation of null\n\nPopulation phylogenies support a closer broad relationship between *M. m. musculus* and *M. m. castaneus* than either with *M. m. domesticus*, while also documenting rapid divergence, incomplete lineage sorting, and gene flow. Thus D1's P1/P2 sister population versus P3 has a plausible broad species-history interpretation, but local geography and introgression still violate a simple panmictic null. D2 reverses this relationship: its P1/P2 are within *musculus*, while P3 is *domesticus*, so the assumed `((P1,P2),P3),O` is broadly compatible at the major-subspecies level, although carrier status and population structure remain confounded. D3 has P1/P2 *castaneus* and P3 *musculus*, which conflicts with the commonly supported CAS+MUS sister relationship for the species-history null; classify it as exploratory for D=0 interpretation. Recent Eurasian whole-genome work finds complex admixture, especially CAS–MUS, and sensitivity of inferred topology to hybrid-sample exclusion. The frozen P3 distributions are broad and not geographically matched to all P1/P2 strata.\n\nThe samples are selected t carriers versus standard arrangement individuals within P1/P2. That is an arrangement-associated population contrast, but VCF genotypes are unphased diploid calls, so it does not isolate t-bearing chromosomes. Exact IDs do not make pseudo-t tree tips the same chromosome copies as the diploid VCF calls.\n\n## Classification\n\n| Design | Rooted quartet | Conventional species null | Structure/admixture concern | t-haplotype-specific? | Use |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| D1 Mmd t vs standard | Yes, with *M. spretus* outgroup | Broadly plausible, conditional | Strong: cross-subspecies P3 and regional ancestry | No | Exploratory arrangement-associated |\n| D2 Mmm t vs standard | Yes, with *M. spretus* outgroup | Broadly plausible at major-subspecies level | Strong: geographic strata and cross-subspecies P3 | No | Exploratory arrangement-associated |\n| D3 Mmc t vs standard | Yes, with *M. spretus* outgroup | No; CAS/MUS sister history conflicts with P1/P2 vs P3 null | Strong: only 3 carriers; India vs broad MUS; admixture | No | Exploratory only; not conventional D=0 test |\n\n## Sources and scope\n\nThis classification is independent of chr17 D and local-tree results. It uses the source-backed sample IDs and geographic metadata plus published population history: [White et al. 2009, genome-wide house-mouse speciation history](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2915842/); [Phifer-Rixey et al. 2022, wild-mouse population structure](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9411160/); [Fujiwara et al. 2022, Eurasian whole-genome structure and admixture](https://academic.oup.com/gbe/article/14/5/evac068/6582302); and [Duvaux et al. 2011, geographic structure/admixture](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3164794/). *M. spretus* is a separate species and established close outgroup for *M. musculus* in the source design; using it roots the quartet but does not establish the ingroup null. No quartets were changed post hoc.\n''')
(A/'haplotype_feasibility.md').write_text('''# Haplotype feasibility assessment\n\nThe Harr et al. AllMouse input used here supplies unphased diploid GT calls. No verified phase blocks or sample-specific chromosome-17 t-homolog calls are in the local input inventory. Kelemen & Vicoso's tree archive contains 5-kb local trees and carrier-tip mapping, not original, validated per-individual phased genotype matrices. Its carrier tips are reconstructed pseudo-t sequences produced from t-associated SNP selection/subtraction; they cannot be treated as independently phased versions of the AllMouse GTs. No directly sample-matched validated chromosome-specific genotypes were identified in the available source inventory.\n\nA published homozygous laboratory t-haplotype resource is not a substitute for wild-carrier haplotypes or sample-matched phase. Without independent read-backed/long-read phasing or validated t-specific linked markers for these exact animals, a t-specific nucleotide ABBA/BABA statistic is not defensible. Assigning carrier heterozygous ALT alleles to t would be circular/incorrect. Keep the corrected statistic labelled population-level diploid allele-frequency D. If independent phase becomes available, predefine chromosome-copy inclusion and phasing QC before testing, retain standard homologs as controls, and compare only exact sample-matched loci; do not infer phase from D or tree topology.\n\nSee [Kelemen & Vicoso 2018](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5753869/) and the exact-quartet report's phase audit.\n''')
